$ tan help
Usage: tan <command> [flags] [args]

Commands:
  init         create a project from a template
  dev          run the app in development mode
  build        build the app for one or more platforms
  run          build and run the app on the iOS Simulator or Android
  package      build, then make installers and archives
  sign         sign a macOS app or disk image (and notarize), or a Windows exe/msi/msix
  keygen       create an Ed25519 key pair for update signing
  release-manifest make the signed update manifest and patches for a release
  sign-update  sign an update manifest with an Ed25519 key
  applinks     write the Universal Links / App Links files for /.well-known/
  generate     generate code (bindings)
  doctor       check the toolchain and platform dependencies
  version      print the version

Run "tan <command> -h" for a command's flags.

$ tan init -h
Usage: tan init [flags] [dir]

Creates a Tango project in dir (which must not exist or be empty). Without
dir, in a terminal, tan init asks for the directory, app name, id, template
and mobile support; -yes skips the questions.

Templates:
  vanilla     plain HTML/CSS/JS, no npm needed
  vanilla-ts  TypeScript + Vite
  react-ts    React + TypeScript + Vite
  vue-ts      Vue + TypeScript + Vite
  shadcn      React + TypeScript + Vite with Tailwind CSS and shadcn/ui components
  ui          self-drawn UI in Go (package ui): no WebView, no npm
  hybrid      WebView page (TypeScript + Vite) with a self-drawn tool panel on top

tan.json gets "permissions": [] and, unless -mobile=false, "ios" and
"android" sections with only minimum OS versions; signing settings are left
for you to add (docs/mobile.md).

Flags:
  -id id
    	app id, reverse-DNS (default: com.example.<name>)
  -local dir
    	use the Tango checkout in dir through a replace directive
  -mobile
    	write the "ios" and "android" sections of tan.json (safe defaults, no signing) (default true)
  -module path
    	Go module path (default: the directory name)
  -name name
    	app display name (default: the directory name)
  -template template
    	project template: vanilla, vanilla-ts, react-ts, vue-ts, shadcn, ui, hybrid (default "vanilla")
  -yes
    	do not ask questions; use the flags and defaults (directory tango-app when none is given)

$ tan dev -h
Usage: tan dev [flags] [-- app args]
  -C dir
    	project dir (where tan.json is) (default ".")
  -device string
    	with -platform: the adb serial or the Simulator UDID or name (default: the only/first one running)
  -hot
    	hot-reload self-drawn UI (uihost) windows instead of restarting the app; TANGO_HOTRELOAD=0 turns it off (default true)
  -no-restore
    	do not restore window positions and sizes when the app restarts
  -platform string
    	run on a phone instead of the desktop: android (a connected device or a running emulator; android/<arch> to force the ABI) or ios-simulator
  -url url
    	use the dev server already running at url instead of starting frontend.dev
  -watch-framework
    	restart the dev host when the Tango sources it links change (local checkouts only) (default true)

$ tan build -h
Usage: tan build [flags]
  -C dir
    	project dir (where tan.json is) (default ".")
  -debug
    	keep symbols and the console window; let the page open the inspector; android: debug APK instead of the signed release
  -o dir
    	output dir (default "build")
  -platform targets
    	comma-separated targets such as darwin/arm64,windows/amd64,linux/amd64 (default: this machine; darwin/universal joins both Mac architectures); mobile: ios, ios-simulator, android, android/arm64,android/amd64
  -xcode
    	ios: also generate an Xcode project in <out>/xcode for debugging

$ tan run -h
Usage: tan run -platform ios-simulator|android [flags]
  -C dir
    	project dir (where tan.json is) (default ".")
  -avd string
    	android: start this emulator (AVD name) when no device is connected (default: the first AVD)
  -device string
    	Simulator name or UDID (ios-simulator), or adb serial (android); default: the booted one
  -o dir
    	output dir (default "build")
  -platform platform
    	platform to run on: ios-simulator or android

$ tan package -h
Usage: tan package [flags]
  -C dir
    	project dir (where tan.json is) (default ".")
  -format format
    	package format: empty for each platform's default (disk image, tarball and AppImage, NSIS installer); msix (Windows targets only); or, for Linux targets, a comma-separated list of tar, appimage, deb, rpm, flatpak
  -o dir
    	output dir (default "build")
  -platform targets
    	comma-separated targets, as for tan build

$ tan sign -h
Usage: tan sign [flags] <App.app|image.dmg> [-- notarytool args]
       tan sign [flags] <app.exe|setup.msi|app.msix|app.appx>...
  -C dir
    	project dir (where tan.json is) (default ".")
  -cert file
    	Windows: PKCS#12 certificate file (default $TANGO_WINDOWS_CERT, else windows.certificate in tan.json)
  -entitlements plist
    	entitlements plist (default macos.entitlements from tan.json)
  -identity identity
    	macOS: codesign identity, e.g. "Developer ID Application: Name (TEAMID)" (default $TANGO_SIGN_IDENTITY)
  -notarize
    	submit to Apple's notary service and staple the ticket
  -password password
    	Windows: certificate password (prefer $TANGO_WINDOWS_CERT_PASSWORD: flags show up in process lists)
  -profile profile
    	notarytool keychain profile (default $TANGO_NOTARY_PROFILE; else $TANGO_APPLE_ID, $TANGO_APPLE_TEAM_ID, $TANGO_APPLE_PASSWORD)
  -timestamp url
    	Windows: RFC 3161 timestamp url (default windows.timestampURL, else http://timestamp.digicert.com)

$ tan keygen -h
Usage: tan keygen [flags]
  -f	overwrite existing key files
  -o name
    	write name.key (private) and name.pub (public) (default "update")

$ tan release-manifest -h
Usage: tan release-manifest [flags]
  -C dir
    	project dir (where tan.json is) (default ".")
  -build dir
    	dir holding the tan build / tan package output (default "build")
  -expires duration
    	how long the manifest stays valid (its "expires"), e.g. 720h; 0 writes none (default 720h0m0s)
  -key file
    	private update key file from tan keygen (default $TANGO_UPDATE_KEY_FILE, or the key itself in $TANGO_UPDATE_KEY)
  -linux format
    	Linux update format: appimage or dir (default appimage when an AppImage was built, else dir)
  -notes notes
    	release notes, or @file to read them from a file
  -o dir
    	output dir for the update files, patches and latest.json (default "release")
  -prev dir
    	dir of an earlier release (its latest.json and files) to make patches from; repeatable
  -url URL
    	base URL the files are served from (default: next to latest.json, as relative URLs)
  -windows format
    	Windows update format: exe (replace the executable) or nsis (run the installer from tan package) (default "exe")

$ tan sign-update -h
Usage: tan sign-update [flags] <manifest.json>
  -expires duration
    	set the manifest's "expires" this long from now, e.g. 720h; 0 keeps the manifest's own (default 720h0m0s)
  -key file
    	private key file from tan keygen (default $TANGO_UPDATE_KEY_FILE, or the key itself in $TANGO_UPDATE_KEY)
  -o file
    	write the signed manifest to file (default: replace the input)

$ tan applinks -h
Usage: tan applinks [flags]
  -C dir
    	project dir (where tan.json is) (default ".")
  -debug
    	android: use the debug keystore's fingerprint (for debug builds only)
  -fingerprint fingerprint
    	SHA-256 signing certificate fingerprint for assetlinks.json; repeat for several keys (Play App Signing: the app signing key from Play Console). Default: android.keystore's key
  -o dir
    	output dir; the files go in <dir>/.well-known (default "build/applinks")
  -platform string
    	which files: ios (apple-app-site-association), android (assetlinks.json) or both (default "ios,android")
  -team ID
    	Apple team ID (default: from ios.provisioningProfile or ios.signIdentity)

$ tan generate bindings -h
Usage: tan generate bindings [flags]
  -C dir
    	project dir (where tan.json is) (default ".")
  -o dir
    	output dir (default <frontend.dir>/src/bindings, or bindings without tan.json)
  -pkg dir
    	Go package dir with the bind.Register calls, relative to the project (default ".")
  -runtime module
    	module that exports call (default "@tangoui/runtime")

$ tan doctor -h
Usage: tan doctor 
